\section{Model access}{

The assistant calls its model from the browser, never through R.
Four blockr options say where the calls go, how they are authenticated and
which models the assistant offers.
A deployment sets each as an R option, such as \code{blockr.assistant_models},
or as an environment variable, such as \code{BLOCKR_ASSISTANT_MODELS}.
\itemize{
\item The blockr option \code{assistant_base_url} gives the base URL that every call
goes to, OpenAI's \verb{https://api.openai.com/v1} by default.
A gateway, such as OpenRouter at \verb{https://openrouter.ai/api/v1}, or a
company's own proxy can take its place.
Anthropic's API is at \verb{https://api.anthropic.com}, without \verb{/v1}, since
the assistant appends \verb{/v1/messages} to it.
\item The blockr option \code{assistant_api} gives the API that the base URL speaks:
OpenAI's Responses API as \code{"openai-responses"}, the default, OpenAI's
Chat Completions API as \code{"openai-completions"}, or Anthropic's Messages
API as \code{"anthropic-messages"}.
The assistant does not work out which API a base URL speaks.
\item The blockr option \code{assistant_api_key} gives the key that every call
carries, or \code{"none"} for a base URL that needs no key, so that no call
carries one.
The assistant reads a key from this option alone, never from a variable
such as \code{OPENAI_API_KEY}, so that a key set for code in R reaches no
browser unless the deployment sets it here.
Any visitor who can use the assistant can read the key from the page.
Where the option is unset, each turn fails with the error
\code{assistant_call_failed}.
\item The blockr option \code{assistant_models} gives the ids of the models offered,
the first being the default, as a character vector or as one string that
separates them by commas, such as \code{"gpt-5.4,gpt-5.4-nano"}.
Where it is unset, the assistant offers the models of the base URL's list
of models that take tools, the first being the default.
}

The assistant asks for the base URL's list with the key as each board
opens.
Where the base URL refuses the key, it raises the error
\code{assistant_call_failed}.
Where the list fails otherwise, or holds no model that takes tools, it
raises the error \code{assistant_models_failed}, and asks again as the user
sends.
A conversation talks to its pick of the model where the deployment offers
it, and to the default otherwise.
}

\section{Model settings}{

A model's settings say whether it reasons, whether it takes images, how
many tokens its context window holds, and how many tokens each call asks
for the reply.
The assistant takes each setting of a model from the first of these that
gives it:
\enumerate{
\item The YAML file that the blockr option \code{assistant_model_settings} names,
such as through the environment variable
\code{BLOCKR_ASSISTANT_MODEL_SETTINGS}.
\item The catalogs of OpenAI's and Anthropic's models of pi, the library that
runs the assistant's turns.
The assistant takes the catalogs' model that the file names in \code{like},
or else their model of the same id.
\item The base URL's list of models, where the assistant offers the models of
the list.
OpenRouter's, Vercel's and Anthropic's lists give each model's context
window, its cap for a reply and whether it takes images.
\item The defaults: the model does not reason, takes images, has a context
window of 128,000 tokens and takes up to 16,384 tokens for a reply.
}

The file gives the settings of each model under the model's id:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{corp-claude:
  like: claude-sonnet-5-5
corp-gpt:
  reasoning: true
  images: false
  context_window: 200000
  max_tokens: 32000
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
\itemize{
\item The setting \code{like} names a model of pi's catalogs, such as
\code{claude-sonnet-5-5}, whose settings the model takes where the file gives
none.
So a model that a company's own proxy serves under a name of its own
takes the settings of the model behind the name.
Where the proxy speaks the API of the model behind the name, those
include what pi sends that API, such as the adaptive thinking that
Anthropic's current models need.
\item The setting \code{reasoning} says whether the model reasons, so that the
assistant asks it to, at the thinking level \code{medium}.
On OpenAI's Chat Completions API, a model whose \code{like} or id names a
model of pi's catalog of OpenAI's models takes the level \code{off} instead.
That API refuses function tools with any reasoning effort but \code{none} for
OpenAI's GPT-5.4 models.
For the level \code{off}, pi sends the effort \code{none} to a model that takes it,
such as \code{gpt-5.4}, and no effort to the others, such as \code{o3}.
\item The setting \code{images} says whether the model takes images.
For a model that takes none, the assistant sends a note in place of each
plot of \code{inspect_results}.
\item The setting \code{context_window} gives the tokens that the model's context
window holds, and \code{max_tokens} the most tokens that each call asks for
the reply.
}

R reads the file as each board opens.
Where R cannot read the file, or where the file gives a value that a
setting does not take, the assistant raises the error
\code{assistant_model_settings_failed}.
It then goes on without the file until the next board opens.
The assistant finds a \code{like} that names no model of pi's catalogs only as
the board's first turn starts, since it loads the catalogs then.
}

\section{Requests}{

The class answers six requests in R, through its method of
\code{\link[blockr.core:plugin_requests]{blockr.core::plugin_requests()}}.
R calls each with the plugin's args by name, and with the blocks that it
reads in \code{reads}, where the request takes them.
The requests that read skills take the app's packages by name, in
\code{packages}, which the plugin sends as R named them in its reply to \code{hello}.
\itemize{
\item The request \code{describe_result} gives what \code{\link[=describe_result]{describe_result()}} writes about
the result of each block that it reads, by block id.
It cuts each text at the blockr option \code{assistant_summary_max_chars},
2000 by default.
\item The request \code{inspect_results} runs the R code in \code{code} with a PNG device
open.
The code runs in an environment whose parent is \code{baseenv()}, which binds
the result of each block that the request reads to the block's id.
The request gives what the code prints in \code{output}, cut at the blockr
option \code{assistant_eval_max_chars}, 16000 by default.
It gives the plots that the code draws in \code{plots}, each a PNG as a base64
string, up to \code{assistant_plot_render_max}, 4 by default.
They are \code{width} by \code{height} pixels, within
\code{assistant_plot_render_range}, 200 to 2000 by default.
Either is \code{assistant_plot_render_px}, 768 by default, where it is left
out.
R refuses the request on a locked board, where the blockr option \code{locked}
is on.
\item The request \code{list_skills} gives the skills (below), each with its \code{name}
and \code{description}, the block classes that it names in \code{blocks}, the
plugin classes that it names in \code{plugins}, and its flags
\code{user-invocable} and \code{disable-model-invocation}.
\item The request \code{read_skill} gives the text of the skill \code{name}, or that of
the file at \code{path} that the skill bundles.
\item The request \code{read_template} gives the text of the file that the blockr
option \code{assistant_template} names, or \code{NULL} where it names none.
\item The request \code{read_model_settings} gives what the YAML file that the
blockr option \code{assistant_model_settings} names holds, or \code{NULL} where it
names none.
}
}

\section{Template}{

Each call to the model sends the system prompt, then the conversation,
then a message of the app's with the board and what the user has on
screen.
The parts below make them up, in the order in which the model reads them.
A deployment's template is the Markdown file that the blockr option
\code{assistant_template} names, which a deployment sets as the R option
\code{blockr.assistant_template} or the environment variable
\code{BLOCKR_ASSISTANT_TEMPLATE}.
It takes the place of the three parts that have an id: it names each part
that it keeps as \verb{<<id>>} on a line of its own, in the order that it wants,
and may add text of its own.
The plugin writes every other part, whatever the template holds.

\tabular{lll}{
\strong{Part} \tab \strong{Id} \tab \strong{What it tells the model} \cr
Role \tab \code{role} \tab
That it is an assistant next to a blockr data analysis board \cr
Build, don't ask \tab \code{build} \tab
To build what the user wants analysed, with defaults from the data,
rather than ask \cr
Style \tab \code{style} \tab To answer concisely \cr
What the app sends you \tab none \tab
That a message of the app's follows the conversation, with the board
and what the user has on screen \cr
Statuses \tab none \tab
What each status marker in the Board section means \cr
Look, don't guess \tab none \tab
That summaries are cut short, so it reads what it relies on rather
than guessing \cr
Edits \tab none \tab
That ids never change, and that changing one needs the user's yes \cr
Skills \tab none \tab
Each skill that names no block class or plugin class, and when to read
one \cr
Board \tab none \tab
The board as it is when the call goes out, in the app's message \cr
Focus \tab none \tab
Which block has focus and what the user has on screen, in the app's
message
}

As a board opens, the plugin has R read the template once.
Where R cannot read it, or it names an id that is none of the three, the
plugin raises the error \code{assistant_template_failed}.
The calls then go on with blockr.assistant's own template until the next
board opens.
}

\section{Skills}{

A skill is a directory that holds a \code{SKILL.md}.
The file's YAML frontmatter gives the skill's \code{name}, which is the
directory's, and its \code{description}.
It may name block classes by their registry ids in \verb{blocks:}, plugin
classes by their ids in \verb{plugins:}, and the packages that the skill needs
in \verb{requires:}.
It may also set the flags \code{user-invocable} and \code{disable-model-invocation}.

R lists the skills of each of the app's packages, from the package's
\code{inst/assistant/skills}, in the order of the packages' names.
It then lists those of the directory that the blockr option
\code{assistant_skills} names.
A skill takes the place of one of the same name that R listed before it.
R does not read a package's \code{inst/skills}, where btw finds the skills that
it gives coding agents.

As a board opens, the plugin has R list the skills once, and the model reads
them through the assistant's tools.
Where R cannot list them, as for a directory in \code{assistant_skills} that does
not exist, the plugin raises the error \code{assistant_skills_failed}.
The assistant then goes on without skills until the next board opens.
}

